Researchers might present the method as a **boundary-adapted approximation scheme** combining an interior discretization with a stable boundary solver:

> We introduce a constructive method for approximating solutions of mixed hyperbolic initial–boundary value problems satisfying the Uniform Lopatinski Condition. The scheme evolves the incoming and outgoing characteristic modes separately in the interior and determines the incoming boundary modes through a discrete boundary Riemann solver. Uniform Lopatinski stability guarantees that this boundary correction is uniquely defined and uniformly bounded with respect to the discretization parameters. Using a Kreiss-type symmetrizer, we derive mesh-independent energy estimates, including control of the boundary trace. Consistency and compactness then yield convergence of the approximate solutions to a weak—or, in the smooth regime, classical—solution of the original system. The construction accommodates variable coefficients and mixed characteristic families and may also provide quantitative error bounds when additional regularity is available.

A full description would normally emphasize:

- decomposition into incoming, outgoing, and possibly characteristic modes;
- an interior finite-volume, front-tracking, or regularized evolution step;
- enforcement of boundary conditions via the Lopatinski-stable boundary operator;
- uniform energy or bounded-variation estimates;
- consistency, compactness, and convergence;
- preservation of admissibility or entropy conditions for nonlinear systems.
